## Microservice Resilience Patterns with Resilience4j

### The Circuit Breaker State Machine

```
              Success rate normal
      ┌─────► [ CLOSED ] ◄─────────┐
      │          │                 │
      │     Failure rate > 50%     │ Wait duration elapsed
      │          │                 │
      │          ▼                 │
      │       [ OPEN ] ────────► [ HALF-OPEN ]
      │    (Fail fast,          (Allow 5 trial calls)
      │     call fallback)         │
      │                            │
      └────────────────────────────┘
```

### Thread Isolation: The Bulkhead Pattern
Prevents a slow or dying downstream service from exhausting all worker threads in your servlet container (e.g. Tomcat's 200 threads):
- **Semaphore Bulkhead**: Limits concurrent calls to a numeric threshold without creating new threads.
- **ThreadPool Bulkhead**: Assigns a dedicated thread pool and queue per external service, strictly containing thread starvation.

### Production Resilience4j Integration in Spring Boot

```java
@Service
public class PaymentGatewayService {

    @CircuitBreaker(name = "paymentClient", fallbackMethod = "processFallback")
    @Bulkhead(name = "paymentClient", type = Bulkhead.Type.THREADPOOL)
    @Retry(name = "paymentClient")
    public CompletableFuture<PaymentReceipt> charge(PaymentRequest request) {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> externalPaymentApi.execute(request));
    }

    public CompletableFuture<PaymentReceipt> processFallback(PaymentRequest request, Throwable ex) {
        log.warn("Payment downstream unavailable. Queuing for async reconciliation. Reason: {}", ex.getMessage());
        outboxQueue.save(request);
        return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(PaymentReceipt.deferred(request.getId()));
    }
}
```

### Order of Execution for Resilience Decorators
```
Retry ──► CircuitBreaker ──► RateLimiter ──► TimeLimiter ──► Bulkhead ──► Business Logic
(Outer)                                                                  (Inner)
```

## Feign Client: Interceptors, Fallbacks & Error Decoding

### What Is Feign?
Feign (Spring Cloud OpenFeign) lets you define HTTP client interfaces declaratively. No RestTemplate boilerplate — just an annotated interface and Spring wires the implementation.

```java
@FeignClient(
    name = "inventory-service",
    url = "${services.inventory.url}",  // or use Eureka: name = "INVENTORY-SERVICE"
    fallbackFactory = InventoryClientFallbackFactory.class
)
public interface InventoryClient {

    @GetMapping("/api/inventory/{productId}")
    InventoryResponse getStock(@PathVariable("productId") String productId);

    @PostMapping("/api/inventory/reserve")
    ReservationResponse reserve(@RequestBody ReservationRequest request);
}
```

### RequestInterceptor: Propagating Auth Headers
Every outgoing Feign call automatically gets the JWT from the incoming request:

```java
@Component
public class JwtPropagationInterceptor implements RequestInterceptor {

    @Override
    public void apply(RequestTemplate template) {
        // Pull JWT from SecurityContext (set by gateway filter)
        Authentication auth = SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuthentication();
        if (auth instanceof JwtAuthenticationToken jwtToken) {
            String bearerToken = jwtToken.getToken().getTokenValue();
            template.header(HttpHeaders.AUTHORIZATION, "Bearer " + bearerToken);
        }
        // Propagate correlation ID for distributed tracing
        String correlationId = MDC.get("traceId");
        if (correlationId != null) {
            template.header("X-Correlation-ID", correlationId);
        }
    }
}
```

### ErrorDecoder: Custom Exception Mapping
Map downstream HTTP error codes to meaningful domain exceptions:

```java
@Component
public class InventoryErrorDecoder implements ErrorDecoder {
    private final ErrorDecoder defaultDecoder = new Default();

    @Override
    public Exception decode(String methodKey, Response response) {
        return switch (response.status()) {
            case 404 -> new ProductNotFoundException("Product not found: " + methodKey);
            case 409 -> new InsufficientStockException("Stock conflict during reservation");
            case 503 -> new ServiceUnavailableException("Inventory service is down");
            default  -> defaultDecoder.decode(methodKey, response);
        };
    }
}
```

### FallbackFactory: Resilience4j Integration
FallbackFactory receives the exception, enabling context-aware fallback logic:

```java
@Component
public class InventoryClientFallbackFactory
        implements FallbackFactory<InventoryClient> {

    @Override
    public InventoryClient create(Throwable cause) {
        return new InventoryClient() {
            @Override
            public InventoryResponse getStock(String productId) {
                log.warn("Inventory fallback for productId={}, cause={}",
                         productId, cause.getMessage());
                return InventoryResponse.unavailable(productId);
            }

            @Override
            public ReservationResponse reserve(ReservationRequest request) {
                throw new ServiceUnavailableException("Cannot reserve: inventory offline");
            }
        };
    }
}
```

### Feign Logging & Timeout Configuration

```yaml
feign:
  client:
    config:
      inventory-service:
        connect-timeout: 2000    # 2s to establish TCP connection
        read-timeout: 5000       # 5s to read response
        logger-level: FULL       # NONE | BASIC | HEADERS | FULL
  circuitbreaker:
    enabled: true

logging:
  level:
    com.example.clients.InventoryClient: DEBUG
```

### Logging Levels Explained
- **NONE**: No logging (production default)
- **BASIC**: Request method, URL, response status, execution time
- **HEADERS**: BASIC + request/response headers
- **FULL**: HEADERS + request/response body (never use in production — logs PII)

## gRPC vs REST vs GraphQL in Microservices

### Protocol Comparison

```
┌──────────────┬──────────────────┬──────────────────┬─────────────────┐
│ Attribute    │ gRPC             │ REST             │ GraphQL          │
├──────────────┼──────────────────┼──────────────────┼─────────────────┤
│ Protocol     │ HTTP/2           │ HTTP/1.1 or 2    │ HTTP/1.1 or 2   │
│ Serialization│ Protobuf (binary)│ JSON/XML (text)  │ JSON (text)     │
│ Type Safety  │ Strong (.proto)  │ None (by default)│ Schema (SDL)    │
│ Streaming    │ Bidirectional    │ SSE/WebSockets   │ Subscriptions   │
│ Debugging    │ Hard (binary)    │ Easy (curl)      │ Moderate        │
│ Browser      │ Not native       │ Native           │ Native          │
│ Code Gen     │ Mandatory        │ Optional (OAS)   │ Optional        │
└──────────────┴──────────────────┴──────────────────┴─────────────────┘
```

### gRPC: Protocol Buffers & HTTP/2

**Protobuf schema** (`payment.proto`):
```protobuf
syntax = "proto3";
package payment;

service PaymentService {
  rpc ChargeCard (ChargeRequest) returns (ChargeResponse);
  rpc StreamTransactions (StreamRequest) returns (stream Transaction);
}

message ChargeRequest {
  string order_id = 1;
  int64 amount_cents = 2;
  string currency = 3;
}

message ChargeResponse {
  string transaction_id = 1;
  bool success = 2;
  string failure_reason = 3;
}
```

**Spring Boot gRPC server** (using `grpc-spring-boot-starter`):
```java
@GrpcService
public class PaymentGrpcService extends PaymentServiceGrpc.PaymentServiceImplBase {

    @Override
    public void chargeCard(ChargeRequest req, StreamObserver<ChargeResponse> observer) {
        try {
            String txId = paymentProcessor.charge(req.getOrderId(), req.getAmountCents());
            observer.onNext(ChargeResponse.newBuilder()
                .setTransactionId(txId)
                .setSuccess(true)
                .build());
            observer.onCompleted();
        } catch (PaymentException e) {
            observer.onError(Status.INTERNAL
                .withDescription(e.getMessage())
                .asRuntimeException());
        }
    }
}
```

### HTTP/2 Advantages for gRPC
- **Multiplexing**: Multiple RPC calls share one TCP connection (no head-of-line blocking)
- **Header compression** (HPACK): Eliminates repeated JSON overhead
- **Binary framing**: Protobuf is 3–10x smaller than equivalent JSON
- **Server push**: Bidirectional streaming without polling

### When to Use Each

**gRPC**: Internal service-to-service calls needing high throughput, low latency, and strong contracts. Ideal for data pipelines, ML inference endpoints, and real-time streaming (stock prices, IoT telemetry).

**REST**: External public APIs consumed by third parties, mobile apps, or browsers. Maximum tooling compatibility. Use when discoverability and simplicity matter more than performance.

**GraphQL**: Client-driven aggregation layers (mobile BFF) where different clients need different data shapes. Eliminates over-fetching and under-fetching. Watch for N+1 query problem — always use DataLoader.

### GraphQL N+1 Problem
```
Query: { orders { customer { name } } }  // 1 query for orders
→ For each order, fetch customer separately  // N queries for N customers
Fix: DataLoader batches N customer lookups into 1 SQL IN clause
```

## Event-Driven Architecture: Domain Events vs Integration Events

### Two Types of Events

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│          Order Bounded Context              │
│                                             │
│  [Order Aggregate]                          │
│       │ raises                              │
│       ▼                                     │
│  [OrderPlaced DomainEvent]  ◄─── Internal   │
│       │ converted to                        │
└───────┼─────────────────────────────────────┘
        │ published as
        ▼
  [OrderCreatedEvent]  ◄─── Integration Event
  (Kafka topic: orders.created)
        │
        ├──► Payment Service (subscribes)
        └──► Inventory Service (subscribes)
```

**Domain Events**: Represent something meaningful that happened within a bounded context. Private to the domain, rich with business meaning, may contain aggregate state. Raised synchronously within a transaction.

**Integration Events**: Cross-service contracts published to a message broker. Must be versioned, minimal, and stable. Treated like a public API — breaking changes require a migration strategy.

### Domain Event in Spring (ApplicationEventPublisher)

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @Transient
    private final List<DomainEvent> domainEvents = new ArrayList<>();

    public void place(List<OrderItem> items) {
        // Business logic
        this.status = OrderStatus.PENDING;
        domainEvents.add(new OrderPlacedDomainEvent(this.id, this.customerId, items));
    }

    @DomainEvents
    public List<DomainEvent> domainEvents() { return domainEvents; }

    @AfterDomainEventPublication
    public void clearDomainEvents() { domainEvents.clear(); }
}

@Component
public class OrderPlacedEventHandler {
    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)
    public void on(OrderPlacedDomainEvent event) {
        // Convert domain event → integration event and publish to Kafka
        // Runs AFTER the DB transaction commits — safe!
        kafkaTemplate.send("orders.created", new OrderCreatedEvent(event.getOrderId()));
    }
}
```

### Schema Evolution with Confluent Schema Registry
Integration events must evolve without breaking consumers:

```json
// v1 OrderCreatedEvent
{ "orderId": "123", "customerId": "c-456", "totalAmount": 99.99 }

// v2 - BACKWARD COMPATIBLE: added optional field with default
{ "orderId": "123", "customerId": "c-456", "totalAmount": 99.99, "currency": "USD" }

// BREAKING CHANGE (avoid!): removed field, changed type
{ "orderId": 123 }  // int instead of string — breaks old consumers!
```

**Compatibility modes**:
- **BACKWARD**: New schema can read old messages (consumer upgrades first)
- **FORWARD**: Old schema can read new messages (producer upgrades first)
- **FULL**: Both directions — safest, most restrictive

### Choreography vs Orchestration (Revisited with Code)

**Choreography** — each service reacts to events independently:
```
OrderCreated → (Payment charges) → PaymentSucceeded → (Inventory reserves) → InventoryReserved → Done
```
Problem: Hard to see the full workflow. 'Where is my order?' requires querying 4 services.

**Orchestration** — central saga orchestrator issues commands:
```java
@SagaOrchestrator
public class PlaceOrderSaga {
    @StartSaga
    @SagaEventHandler(associationProperty = "orderId")
    public void handle(OrderCreatedEvent event) {
        commandGateway.send(new ChargePaymentCommand(event.getOrderId(), event.getAmount()));
    }
    // ... compensating handlers for payment failure
}
```

## Idempotency Keys & At-Least-Once Delivery

### Why Messages Are Delivered At-Least-Once
In distributed systems, networks are unreliable. When a producer sends a message and the broker crashes before ACKing, the producer retries. The broker may have already persisted the message — resulting in a **duplicate**.

```
Producer ──sends msg──► Broker ──persists──► Disk
   ▲                       │
   │      ACK lost!        │
   └─── retry (duplicate!) ┘
```

Kafka guarantees **at-least-once** delivery by default (acks=all, retries>0). Exactly-once requires extra configuration.

### Idempotency Key Pattern (REST APIs)
Clients generate a unique key per logical operation. Servers store the result of the first execution and return the cached response on retries:

```java
@RestController
public class PaymentController {

    @PostMapping("/api/payments")
    public ResponseEntity<PaymentResponse> charge(
            @RequestHeader("Idempotency-Key") String idempotencyKey,
            @RequestBody PaymentRequest request) {

        // Check deduplication table first
        Optional<PaymentResponse> cached = idempotencyStore.find(idempotencyKey);
        if (cached.isPresent()) {
            return ResponseEntity.ok(cached.get());  // Return same response
        }

        PaymentResponse response = paymentService.charge(request);

        // Persist idempotency key + response atomically
        idempotencyStore.save(idempotencyKey, response, Duration.ofHours(24));
        return ResponseEntity.ok(response);
    }
}
```

### Deduplication Table (Database Unique Constraint)

```sql
CREATE TABLE payment_idempotency (
    idempotency_key VARCHAR(255) PRIMARY KEY,
    response_body   JSONB NOT NULL,
    created_at      TIMESTAMP DEFAULT NOW(),
    expires_at      TIMESTAMP NOT NULL
);
-- Unique constraint guarantees atomic deduplication even under concurrent retries
```

### Kafka Idempotent Producer
Enables exactly-once semantics at the producer level (within a single producer session):

```yaml
spring:
  kafka:
    producer:
      acks: all                    # Wait for all replicas to persist
      retries: 2147483647          # Max retries
      properties:
        enable.idempotence: true   # PID + sequence numbers prevent duplicates
        max.in.flight.requests.per.connection: 5
```

The broker tracks `(ProducerID, PartitionID, SequenceNumber)` — duplicate sequences are silently dropped.

### Idempotent Consumer Pattern
Even with an idempotent producer, consumers can receive the same message twice (consumer rebalance, pod restart). Make consumer processing idempotent:

```java
@KafkaListener(topics = "orders.created", groupId = "inventory-service")
public void onOrderCreated(OrderCreatedEvent event) {
    // Upsert with 'ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING' — safe to run twice
    inventoryRepository.reserveIfNotExists(
        event.getOrderId(), event.getItems()
    );
}
```

### Retry with Exponential Backoff + Jitter
Naive constant-interval retries cause thundering herd when all services recover simultaneously:

```java
// Exponential backoff with jitter
long delay = (long) (Math.pow(2, attemptNumber) * 1000) // base: 2^n seconds
           + ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextLong(0, 1000); // +0-1s jitter
```

Spring Retry handles this declaratively:
```java
@Retryable(maxAttempts = 5, backoff = @Backoff(delay = 1000, multiplier = 2, random = true))
public void processPayment(PaymentRequest request) { ... }
```

## Microservices Security: JWT Propagation & OAuth2 with Keycloak

### End-to-End Security Architecture

```
[Mobile App]
    │ 1. Login (Authorization Code Flow)
    ▼
[Keycloak] ──── issues JWT ────►  [Mobile App]
    │
    │ 2. API call with Bearer JWT
    ▼
[API Gateway]
    │ 3. Validate JWT signature (JWK endpoint)
    │ 4. Forward JWT in Authorization header
    ▼
[Order Service]
    │ 5. Service-to-service call (client credentials)
    ▼
[Payment Service]
    │ 6. mTLS (mutual TLS) — no JWT needed for internal
    ▼
[Fraud Detection Service]
```

### Bearer Token Propagation Between Services

```java
// Spring Security Resource Server configuration
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        return http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/actuator/health").permitAll()
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .oauth2ResourceServer(oauth2 -> oauth2
                .jwt(jwt -> jwt.jwtAuthenticationConverter(jwtConverter()))
            )
            .build();
    }

    @Bean
    public JwtAuthenticationConverter jwtConverter() {
        JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter conv = new JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter();
        conv.setAuthoritiesClaimName("realm_access.roles");  // Keycloak realm roles
        conv.setAuthorityPrefix("ROLE_");
        JwtAuthenticationConverter jwtConv = new JwtAuthenticationConverter();
        jwtConv.setJwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter(conv);
        return jwtConv;
    }
}
```

```yaml
# application.yml — point to Keycloak JWKS endpoint
spring:
  security:
    oauth2:
      resourceserver:
        jwt:
          jwk-set-uri: http://keycloak:8080/realms/my-realm/protocol/openid-connect/certs
```

### Service-to-Service Auth: Client Credentials Flow
Services authenticating with each other (not on behalf of a user) use the **Client Credentials** flow:

```java
@Configuration
public class OAuth2ClientConfig {

    @Bean
    public OAuth2AuthorizedClientManager authorizedClientManager(
            ClientRegistrationRepository clients,
            OAuth2AuthorizedClientService clientService) {
        return new AuthorizedClientServiceOAuth2AuthorizedClientManager(
            clients,
            new ClientCredentialsOAuth2AuthorizedClientProvider());
    }

    @Bean
    public WebClient paymentServiceClient(OAuth2AuthorizedClientManager manager) {
        ServletOAuth2AuthorizedClientExchangeFilterFunction filter =
            new ServletOAuth2AuthorizedClientExchangeFilterFunction(manager);
        filter.setDefaultClientRegistrationId("payment-service");
        return WebClient.builder().apply(filter.oauth2Configuration()).build();
    }
}
```

```yaml
spring:
  security:
    oauth2:
      client:
        registration:
          payment-service:
            client-id: order-service
            client-secret: ${PAYMENT_CLIENT_SECRET}
            authorization-grant-type: client_credentials
            scope: payment:write
        provider:
          payment-service:
            token-uri: http://keycloak:8080/realms/my-realm/protocol/openid-connect/token
```

### mTLS for Internal Service Communication
mTLS eliminates the need for application-level tokens between trusted internal services. Both parties present certificates; the TLS handshake verifies both sides.

```yaml
# Istio automatically handles mTLS via PeerAuthentication policy
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: PeerAuthentication
metadata:
  name: default
spec:
  mtls:
    mode: STRICT  # No plaintext traffic allowed between pods
```

### Keycloak Realm Setup Checklist
- Create realm per environment (dev/staging/prod)
- Create client per microservice
- Configure **realm roles** (admin, user) and **client roles** (payment:write)
- Map roles to JWT via **Mapper** configuration
- Set token expiry (15m access token, 24h refresh token)

## Microservices Testing Pyramid

### The Testing Pyramid for Microservices

```
              ┌──────────┐
              │   E2E    │  Docker Compose, Slow, Few
             ┌┴──────────┴┐
             │  Contract  │  Spring Cloud Contract / Pact
            ┌┴────────────┴┐
            │ Integration  │  @SpringBootTest + Testcontainers
           ┌┴──────────────┴┐
           │      Unit      │  Mockito, Fast, Many
           └────────────────┘
```

### Unit Tests (Mockito)
Fast, isolated, no Spring context:
```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class OrderServiceTest {

    @Mock private OrderRepository orderRepository;
    @Mock private PaymentClient paymentClient;
    @InjectMocks private OrderService orderService;

    @Test
    void placeOrder_whenPaymentSucceeds_shouldCreateOrder() {
        // Arrange
        given(paymentClient.charge(any())).willReturn(new PaymentResponse("txn-123", true));
        given(orderRepository.save(any())).willAnswer(inv -> inv.getArgument(0));

        // Act
        Order order = orderService.placeOrder(new OrderRequest("p-1", 2, "c-1"));

        // Assert
        assertThat(order.getStatus()).isEqualTo(OrderStatus.CONFIRMED);
        verify(orderRepository).save(argThat(o -> o.getPaymentRef().equals("txn-123")));
    }
}
```

### Integration Tests (Testcontainers)
Spin up real PostgreSQL and Kafka in Docker for integration tests:
```java
@SpringBootTest
@Testcontainers
class OrderRepositoryIntegrationTest {

    @Container
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:16")
        .withDatabaseName("orders_test")
        .withUsername("test")
        .withPassword("test");

    @Container
    static KafkaContainer kafka = new KafkaContainer(
        DockerImageName.parse("confluentinc/cp-kafka:7.6.0")
    );

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void configureProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
        registry.add("spring.datasource.url", postgres::getJdbcUrl);
        registry.add("spring.kafka.bootstrap-servers", kafka::getBootstrapServers);
    }

    @Test
    void saveOrder_shouldPersistAndPublishEvent() {
        // Test with real DB and real Kafka
    }
}
```

### Consumer-Driven Contract Tests (Spring Cloud Contract)
**Producer side** — define contract in Groovy/YAML:
```groovy
// src/test/resources/contracts/order-created.groovy
Contract.make {
    request {
        method POST()
        url '/api/orders'
        body(customerId: 'c-123', productId: 'p-456', quantity: 2)
    }
    response {
        status 201
        body(orderId: regex('[a-f0-9-]{36}'), status: 'PENDING')
        headers { contentType(applicationJson()) }
    }
}
```

Spring Cloud Contract auto-generates a stub JAR. **Consumer side** uses the stub to test integration without the real service running:
```java
@SpringBootTest
@AutoConfigureStubRunner(
    ids = "com.example:order-service:+:stubs:8080",
    stubsMode = StubRunnerProperties.StubsMode.CLASSPATH
)
class InventoryConsumerContractTest {
    @Test
    void shouldCreateOrder_andReceive201() {
        // Stub server is running at localhost:8080 with pre-defined responses
        ResponseEntity<OrderResponse> response = restTemplate
            .postForEntity("http://localhost:8080/api/orders", request, OrderResponse.class);
        assertThat(response.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.CREATED);
    }
}
```

### E2E Tests (Docker Compose)
```yaml
# docker-compose.test.yml
services:
  order-service:
    image: order-service:latest
    depends_on: [postgres, kafka, payment-service]
  payment-service:
    image: payment-service:latest
  postgres:
    image: postgres:16
  kafka:
    image: confluentinc/cp-kafka:7.6.0
```
Run with Testcontainers `DockerComposeContainer` or `docker compose up` in CI pipeline.

### Pact — Alternative to Spring Cloud Contract
Pact is language-agnostic (works with Node.js consumers, Java producers). Consumer generates a pact file, uploads to Pact Broker, producer verifies against it in CI.

## Async Communication Patterns in Microservices

### Sync vs Async: The Fundamental Trade-off

```
        Synchronous (REST/gRPC)        Asynchronous (Kafka/RabbitMQ)
        ─────────────────────          ──────────────────────────────
        + Simple, easy to debug        + Temporal decoupling
        + Immediate response            + Better fault isolation
        + Easy error propagation        + Natural backpressure
        - Temporal coupling             - Eventual consistency only
        - Cascading failures            - Complex error handling
        - Availability chain            - Hard to debug message flows

  USE WHEN: User needs immediate       USE WHEN: Cross-service state
  response (checkout total, search)    changes, batch processing,
  or strong consistency required.      fanout, audit logging.
```

### Pattern 1: Fire-and-Forget
Send an event and don't wait for a response. The sender is decoupled from all consumers:

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    public Order placeOrder(OrderRequest request) {
        Order order = orderRepository.save(Order.from(request));

        // Fire-and-forget: don't block on notification or analytics
        kafkaTemplate.send("orders.created", OrderCreatedEvent.from(order));
        // Returns immediately — notification service handles asynchronously
        return order;
    }
}
```

### Pattern 2: Request-Reply over Messaging (Correlation ID)
For async operations that need a response, use a correlation ID and a reply topic:

```java
// Requester (Order Service)
public CompletableFuture<FraudCheckResult> checkFraud(OrderRequest request) {
    String correlationId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
    CompletableFuture<FraudCheckResult> future = new CompletableFuture<>();
    pendingRequests.put(correlationId, future);  // Store pending future

    FraudCheckRequest msg = new FraudCheckRequest(correlationId, request.getCustomerId());
    kafkaTemplate.send("fraud.check.requests", msg);

    // Timeout after 5 seconds
    return future.orTimeout(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
                 .whenComplete((r, e) -> pendingRequests.remove(correlationId));
}

// Handle reply from Fraud Service
@KafkaListener(topics = "fraud.check.replies", groupId = "order-service")
public void onFraudCheckReply(FraudCheckResult result) {
    CompletableFuture<FraudCheckResult> future =
        pendingRequests.get(result.getCorrelationId());
    if (future != null) {
        future.complete(result);
    }
}
```

### Pattern 3: Publish-Subscribe
One producer, multiple independent consumers. Perfect for events that multiple services care about:

```
[OrderCreatedEvent]
        │
        ▼
  [Kafka Topic: orders.created]
        │
   ┌────┼────────┐
   ▼    ▼        ▼
[Email] [Loyalty] [Analytics]
 Service  Points    Pipeline
```

Kafka consumer groups allow multiple independent consumer groups to each receive every message:
```java
// Email service — separate consumer group
@KafkaListener(topics = "orders.created", groupId = "email-service")
public void sendConfirmation(OrderCreatedEvent event) { ... }

// Loyalty service — different consumer group, same messages
@KafkaListener(topics = "orders.created", groupId = "loyalty-service")
public void awardPoints(OrderCreatedEvent event) { ... }
```

### Kafka for Event Streaming vs RabbitMQ for Work Queues

| Aspect | Apache Kafka | RabbitMQ |
|--------|-------------|----------|
| Model | Log (events retained) | Queue (messages consumed and deleted) |
| Replay | Yes — replay from offset | No — message gone after consume |
| Ordering | Per-partition | Per-queue |
| Throughput | Millions/sec | ~50-100k/sec |
| Routing | Topic/partition | Exchange → Queue (flexible routing keys) |
| Best For | Event streaming, audit trail, CQRS | Task queues, RPC, work distribution |

### Temporal Coupling vs Decoupling
**Coupled (REST)**: `Order Service` → `Payment Service` must both be UP simultaneously. If Payment is down, the order fails.

**Decoupled (Kafka)**: Order Service publishes `PaymentRequested` event. Payment Service processes it when available. Order Service continues even if Payment is temporarily down — events buffer in Kafka.
